1
00:00:06,362 --> 00:00:07,082
[SPEAKER_02]: Where do you have in mind?

2
00:00:07,663 --> 00:00:12,526
[SPEAKER_02]: I was thinking the difference between samatha and vipassana.

3
00:00:13,406 --> 00:00:18,970
[SPEAKER_01]: Where do you think the difference is?

4
00:00:23,973 --> 00:00:30,677
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, I think it... it relates to what we've been talking about in previous talks, in recent talks.

5
00:00:31,397 --> 00:00:34,119
[SPEAKER_02]: So, eh... samatha...

6
00:00:36,538 --> 00:00:48,723
[SPEAKER_02]: It seems to be more about unifying and composing the mind, recognizing the mind.

7
00:00:49,123 --> 00:00:53,805
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you compose your mind without understanding what the mind is?

8
00:00:53,825 --> 00:00:54,005
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

9
00:00:55,405 --> 00:01:04,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you compose the mind without seeing the mind and just by following an external set of prescriptions of how to compose your mind?

10
00:01:04,529 --> 00:01:04,709
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

11
00:01:06,078 --> 00:01:07,078
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that even conceivable?

12
00:01:07,318 --> 00:01:07,578
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

13
00:01:08,999 --> 00:01:13,940
[SPEAKER_01]: So, can you then compose your mind spontaneous?

14
00:01:15,641 --> 00:01:15,861
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

15
00:01:16,821 --> 00:01:19,282
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you compose your mind unknowingly?

16
00:01:20,022 --> 00:01:20,863
[SPEAKER_01]: Unknowingly, no.

17
00:01:22,043 --> 00:01:23,864
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what do all these things have in common then?

18
00:01:23,884 --> 00:01:25,184
[SPEAKER_01]: Vipassana.

19
00:01:28,305 --> 00:01:30,886
[SPEAKER_01]: So, can you compose the mind without understanding of the mind?

20
00:01:31,506 --> 00:01:31,726
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

21
00:01:32,786 --> 00:01:34,787
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's no samatha without vipassana?

22
00:01:37,840 --> 00:01:45,625
[SPEAKER_01]: And the reason why I ask that is because, you know, I don't know how many times I myself heard it from different people.

23
00:01:45,645 --> 00:01:50,648
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, a general contemporary view of... oh, you do your vipassana.

24
00:01:51,069 --> 00:01:51,269
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

25
00:01:51,529 --> 00:01:54,951
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not referring to the meditation technique of vipassana, but, like, study.

26
00:01:54,971 --> 00:02:04,197
[SPEAKER_01]: You do your study, and now it's time for formal meditation, whereby you go and you follow, like, watch your nostrils, and somehow what you studied

27
00:02:04,895 --> 00:02:11,200
[SPEAKER_01]: will miraculously fit into this experience of non-thinking and observing sensations.

28
00:02:12,681 --> 00:02:15,984
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's absolutely two completely different things, aren't there?

29
00:02:16,144 --> 00:02:22,329
[SPEAKER_01]: If you see no relationship between samatha and vipassana, they are not two yokes of the same thing as the Buddha described.

30
00:02:22,349 --> 00:02:24,010
[SPEAKER_01]: They are completely two different yokes.

31
00:02:24,871 --> 00:02:26,612
[SPEAKER_01]: They're not even yokes because they're not pulling anything.

32
00:02:26,652 --> 00:02:28,674
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, they are yokes of the wrong view, I suppose.

33
00:02:29,592 --> 00:02:34,638
[SPEAKER_01]: Wrong idea of samatha and wrong idea of vipassana are reinforcing your wrong views of what practice is.

34
00:02:35,519 --> 00:02:40,185
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, you are yoking yourself, just the other way.

35
00:02:40,645 --> 00:02:46,212
[SPEAKER_02]: My experience, it was maybe more, you do your samatha,

36
00:02:47,606 --> 00:02:49,168
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you need to calm your mind.

37
00:02:49,448 --> 00:02:49,628
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

38
00:02:49,688 --> 00:03:01,860
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, once it's calm enough, you now direct your mind to the nature of... And, and, funnily enough, all of that is completely refuted by the Buddha in, in Samādhi-tsutta.

39
00:03:03,422 --> 00:03:09,264
[SPEAKER_01]: There's absolutely no such thing as right meditation without a right view.

40
00:03:10,084 --> 00:03:11,145
[SPEAKER_01]: It is inconceivable.

41
00:03:11,845 --> 00:03:20,508
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, here you are, starting your practice, thinking what you do as a meditation is the meditation that pertains to the meditation of enlightenment or whatever it is teaching.

42
00:03:21,088 --> 00:03:25,530
[SPEAKER_01]: Knowingly, at the same time, you know that you have no right view, but you're thinking, my meditation is right.

43
00:03:26,071 --> 00:03:46,801
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is that... what would be the sole criteria, then, for those people, for people who engage in such a contradictory view, yet adhere to a certain method or technique of the meditation, knowing at the same time that they don't have the right view, they don't know what the mind is, they don't understand the nature of the mind, yet they're practicing calming of the mind.

44
00:03:47,341 --> 00:03:48,942
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the criteria for that calming?

45
00:03:50,214 --> 00:03:51,855
[SPEAKER_01]: How nice it feels!

46
00:03:51,995 --> 00:03:53,156
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasure, exactly.

47
00:03:53,716 --> 00:03:56,237
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what we had said again and again.

48
00:03:57,098 --> 00:04:00,039
[SPEAKER_01]: Without the right view, you'll be just meditating with your own sensuality.

49
00:04:00,979 --> 00:04:02,980
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot possibly not meditate without it.

50
00:04:03,481 --> 00:04:05,382
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's not a meditation that he approves of.

51
00:04:07,263 --> 00:04:07,483
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

52
00:04:08,203 --> 00:04:16,567
[SPEAKER_01]: So, think of samatha as calm vipassana and think of vipassana as active samatha.

53
00:04:20,605 --> 00:04:23,446
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

54
00:04:24,146 --> 00:04:44,274
[SPEAKER_02]: But, it seems to me that I still see two, like, sort of different directions, or like, slight different perspective, or orientation, involved in the samatha-vipassana distinction.

55
00:04:44,294 --> 00:04:44,434
[SPEAKER_01]: Go on.

56
00:04:44,454 --> 00:04:44,734
[SPEAKER_02]: Describe.

57
00:05:02,057 --> 00:05:06,100
[SPEAKER_02]: All that can manifest, manifests as an image.

58
00:05:07,781 --> 00:05:12,245
[SPEAKER_02]: And any notion of something outside of that is also another image.

59
00:05:13,445 --> 00:05:18,529
[SPEAKER_02]: Clarifying that mind comes first, that direction seems to be about what samatha is.

60
00:05:22,690 --> 00:05:27,212
[SPEAKER_02]: Whereas, I would say, vipassana seems to be more...

61
00:05:29,513 --> 00:05:39,997
[SPEAKER_02]: I guess they are totally linked, but vipassana is more about the fact that whatever image, whatever has manifested...

62
00:05:40,298 --> 00:05:45,080
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry, you just said that clarifying that thought is what samādhi is.

63
00:05:45,200 --> 00:05:46,220
[SPEAKER_01]: Clarifying that thought.

64
00:05:46,420 --> 00:05:47,320
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what vipassana is.

65
00:05:48,101 --> 00:05:51,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Clarifying that mind comes first, understanding the nature of

66
00:05:51,496 --> 00:06:00,363
[SPEAKER_01]: In a reasonably enduring image, that any other relationship, any other movement, any other thought in regard to it, must be within the fundamentally manifested enduring image.

67
00:06:00,764 --> 00:06:01,684
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what vipassana is.

68
00:06:01,765 --> 00:06:02,905
[SPEAKER_02]: You're just discerning it.

69
00:06:05,941 --> 00:06:11,025
[SPEAKER_01]: And see, they're so linked together, that you thought you were describing one, while you were describing the other.

70
00:06:11,905 --> 00:06:28,077
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I keep thinking of vipassana as being more about the fact that I didn't, I can't own this image, this, whatever, whatever, whatever has manifested, sort of, I was subjected to it, rather than I create, you know, I can't own this.

71
00:06:29,338 --> 00:06:30,119
[SPEAKER_02]: That, to me, seems...

72
00:06:30,319 --> 00:06:31,220
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what's the summit then?

73
00:06:32,640 --> 00:06:36,368
[SPEAKER_02]: So, samādhi is more about including the totality of your experience.

74
00:06:36,869 --> 00:06:37,430
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, not...

75
00:06:41,587 --> 00:06:42,387
[SPEAKER_02]: Including what?

76
00:06:42,707 --> 00:06:44,208
[SPEAKER_02]: Including, like, so, as an image.

77
00:06:44,388 --> 00:06:44,508
[SPEAKER_02]: As...

78
00:06:44,668 --> 00:06:46,028
[SPEAKER_02]: Including what you just clarified.

79
00:06:46,128 --> 00:06:46,749
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, exactly.

80
00:06:46,929 --> 00:06:47,909
[SPEAKER_02]: See, it's not the opposite direction.

81
00:06:47,929 --> 00:06:49,249
[SPEAKER_02]: No, it's not the opposite direction.

82
00:06:49,349 --> 00:06:50,610
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't have one without the other.

83
00:06:50,670 --> 00:06:52,951
[SPEAKER_02]: Different emphasis of the same... Yeah, that's what I'm... yeah.

84
00:06:53,151 --> 00:06:54,331
[SPEAKER_02]: But there is a different emphasis there.

85
00:06:54,471 --> 00:06:55,091
[SPEAKER_01]: No, of course.

86
00:06:55,111 --> 00:06:55,831
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why there are two.

87
00:06:56,452 --> 00:06:57,952
[SPEAKER_01]: Two balls of one yoke.

88
00:06:58,552 --> 00:06:59,753
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody says they're one thing.

89
00:07:00,293 --> 00:07:04,334
[SPEAKER_01]: But, literally, if one ball pulls harder, the other one is pulled by it.

90
00:07:04,354 --> 00:07:05,634
[SPEAKER_01]: They cannot be separated.

91
00:07:05,794 --> 00:07:06,995
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no yoke without two.

92
00:07:08,710 --> 00:07:16,212
[SPEAKER_01]: But, what general attitude is, it's basically two separate balls with two separate yokes.

93
00:07:16,232 --> 00:07:17,852
[SPEAKER_01]: And, like, somehow they magically connect.

94
00:07:17,993 --> 00:07:19,633
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, let's... let me try and put it another way.

95
00:07:19,653 --> 00:07:22,894
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only way to discern the two is within one.

96
00:07:23,674 --> 00:07:24,394
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what it was said.

97
00:07:24,434 --> 00:07:26,935
[SPEAKER_01]: Two balls are tied with one yoke.

98
00:07:27,535 --> 00:07:31,636
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you are approaching... you're trying to discern yoke by chasing two different balls, you're not.

99
00:07:31,816 --> 00:07:32,036
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

100
00:07:32,356 --> 00:07:34,077
[SPEAKER_01]: First, you need to understand what yoking is.

101
00:07:35,223 --> 00:07:36,784
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you'll see the two aspects of it.

102
00:07:37,024 --> 00:07:38,344
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you'll know where the two balls are.

103
00:07:38,684 --> 00:07:42,366
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you'll be practicing samatha, developing vipassana, vipassana, then maintain samatha.

104
00:07:42,386 --> 00:07:42,626
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

105
00:07:43,126 --> 00:07:43,346
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay.

106
00:07:44,207 --> 00:07:48,188
[SPEAKER_01]: And some of the most accurately would be what you just said, discernment is.

107
00:07:48,248 --> 00:07:50,209
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you discern the image has to endure first.

108
00:07:50,789 --> 00:07:57,132
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter the secondary action, choices, intentions, and so on, that come... will be within and you stop assuming outside of it.

109
00:07:57,392 --> 00:07:57,532
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

110
00:07:57,592 --> 00:07:59,953
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your now clarified, discerned context.

111
00:08:00,433 --> 00:08:01,374
[SPEAKER_01]: That also endures.

112
00:08:02,041 --> 00:08:06,845
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, the things that have arisen on their own were enduring, that allowed you to discern the nature of enduring.

113
00:08:07,405 --> 00:08:09,067
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, your discernment endures.

114
00:08:09,527 --> 00:08:12,489
[SPEAKER_01]: Maintaining the endurance of your discernment is what Samatha is.

115
00:08:13,791 --> 00:08:16,313
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why your discernment will come to fulfillment.

116
00:08:17,213 --> 00:08:21,116
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why in jhānas, you fully fulfill your understanding of things.

117
00:08:22,858 --> 00:08:24,139
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you come there with the right view.

118
00:08:25,626 --> 00:08:27,507
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's exactly what's enduring, the clarity.

119
00:08:27,987 --> 00:08:49,154
[SPEAKER_02]: But could you say, then, could you say that the the recognizing the unknown ability of what's manifested, is more an aspect of vipassana, or the direction of vipassana, but, eh...

120
00:08:51,098 --> 00:08:52,600
[SPEAKER_02]: No, you're not applying it.

121
00:09:04,073 --> 00:09:04,393
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay.

122
00:09:04,873 --> 00:09:06,354
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're applying it, it means you're doing it.

123
00:09:06,374 --> 00:09:08,135
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not discerning it on account of the reason.

124
00:09:08,976 --> 00:09:13,118
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the reason why it feels like it's a separate direction is because you keep interjecting yourself.

125
00:09:13,178 --> 00:09:14,239
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

126
00:09:14,299 --> 00:09:15,119
[SPEAKER_01]: It's doing something.

127
00:09:15,299 --> 00:09:21,323
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you have the discernment of vipassana you talk about, within that, discern the samatha.

128
00:09:21,503 --> 00:09:22,443
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't do it elsewhere.

129
00:09:22,623 --> 00:09:22,844
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

130
00:09:23,124 --> 00:09:23,744
[SPEAKER_01]: That's my point.

131
00:09:24,084 --> 00:09:30,288
[SPEAKER_01]: See, it's not accurate even on that entirely internal, personal, slight shift of your attention.

132
00:09:30,488 --> 00:09:30,708
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

133
00:09:30,928 --> 00:09:36,174
[SPEAKER_01]: Let alone how inaccurate it is when you do it as a separate thing, my study and my meditation.

134
00:09:36,915 --> 00:09:37,756
[SPEAKER_01]: It's earth and sky.

135
00:09:38,056 --> 00:09:38,216
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

136
00:09:39,618 --> 00:09:42,961
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when I, like, when we usually do it, oh, how are you feeling right now?

137
00:09:42,981 --> 00:09:44,603
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the presently enduring feeling?

138
00:09:47,046 --> 00:09:48,367
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all right.

139
00:09:49,228 --> 00:09:50,690
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what are the characteristics?

140
00:09:51,785 --> 00:09:53,146
[SPEAKER_01]: of the present enduring feeling.

141
00:09:53,666 --> 00:09:55,908
[SPEAKER_01]: The characteristics... Just your current mood.

142
00:09:55,988 --> 00:09:58,610
[SPEAKER_01]: Describe me the characteristics of it.

143
00:09:59,411 --> 00:10:00,932
[SPEAKER_01]: In the nature, not in the content.

144
00:10:01,112 --> 00:10:02,193
[SPEAKER_01]: Right now, it feels a bit bland.

145
00:10:03,093 --> 00:10:03,554
[SPEAKER_01]: Bland.

146
00:10:03,654 --> 00:10:06,136
[SPEAKER_01]: What are the kind of symptoms of blandness, so to speak?

147
00:10:06,156 --> 00:10:10,059
[SPEAKER_01]: A lack of... A lack of... A lack.

148
00:10:10,139 --> 00:10:10,839
[SPEAKER_02]: A certain lack.

149
00:10:10,879 --> 00:10:11,319
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay.

150
00:10:11,720 --> 00:10:17,564
[SPEAKER_01]: So, see, now you're basically discerning and looking and lifting up and looking around of one thing that endures.

151
00:10:17,664 --> 00:10:17,924
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

152
00:10:18,185 --> 00:10:21,327
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, imagine I ask you, what is your current present enduring feeling?

153
00:10:22,570 --> 00:10:23,772
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'm asking you that again now.

154
00:10:24,313 --> 00:10:24,673
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

155
00:10:24,794 --> 00:10:26,136
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is it?

156
00:10:26,236 --> 00:10:26,617
[SPEAKER_01]: Neutral.

157
00:10:26,997 --> 00:10:27,678
[SPEAKER_01]: Neutral feeling.

158
00:10:27,738 --> 00:10:28,079
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

159
00:10:28,099 --> 00:10:30,843
[SPEAKER_01]: So... And now?

160
00:10:31,424 --> 00:10:33,027
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the presently enduring feeling?

161
00:10:33,683 --> 00:10:34,163
[SPEAKER_01]: Neutral.

162
00:10:34,423 --> 00:10:34,803
[SPEAKER_01]: Neutral.

163
00:10:34,964 --> 00:10:35,304
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

164
00:10:35,324 --> 00:10:42,126
[SPEAKER_01]: So, we keep talking about, and I keep asking you a few times, you're not actively thinking about the present enduring, but you're not ignoring it either.

165
00:10:42,207 --> 00:10:42,367
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

166
00:10:42,427 --> 00:10:43,427
[SPEAKER_01]: It's there, enduring.

167
00:10:43,647 --> 00:10:43,867
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

168
00:10:44,207 --> 00:10:45,548
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the samatha aspect of it.

169
00:10:45,808 --> 00:10:46,028
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

170
00:10:46,068 --> 00:10:50,130
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not actively engaging with what's enduring, but you're still aware of its enduring.

171
00:10:50,150 --> 00:10:50,490
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

172
00:10:50,510 --> 00:10:59,013
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why mindfulness, developed correctly, result in proper, that's why samādhi developed correctly, result in fulfilling all four foundations of mindfulness.

173
00:10:59,093 --> 00:11:02,675
[SPEAKER_01]: And no one would, no one would think that you could do vipassana without mindfulness.

174
00:11:03,515 --> 00:11:04,595
[SPEAKER_02]: Nobody would think what you mean.

175
00:11:04,956 --> 00:11:05,436
[SPEAKER_02]: No one would...

176
00:11:05,496 --> 00:11:11,118
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't think anyone would... and no one would argue... that you can do vipassana without mindfulness.

177
00:11:11,378 --> 00:11:14,139
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why you can't have too much of mindfulness either, in the same way.

178
00:11:14,339 --> 00:11:20,582
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a basis for what you want to discern, or it's a basis for maintaining the enduring image of what you discerned.

179
00:11:21,962 --> 00:11:25,264
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, mindfulness has to be there, and you can't do too much of it.

180
00:11:25,784 --> 00:11:28,125
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can, as the Buddha said, you can do too much of vipassana.

181
00:11:29,701 --> 00:11:30,962
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can do too much of samādhi.

182
00:11:31,002 --> 00:11:35,584
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, when you don't make enough effort to think about the characteristics which remain to a degree unclear.

183
00:11:35,944 --> 00:11:36,165
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

184
00:11:37,125 --> 00:11:41,547
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, you can be thinking a bit too much to a degree that you're becoming a bit nervous and restless and not calm.

185
00:11:42,608 --> 00:11:53,213
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, you kind of... because you're so engaged with characteristics of whatever is presently enduring, you're forgetting the present enduring thing is enduring there on its own and it doesn't require your effort of upholding it.

186
00:11:53,654 --> 00:11:54,674
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you get a bit restless.

187
00:11:55,295 --> 00:11:57,756
[SPEAKER_01]: But either way, with the right view, it will even itself out.

188
00:11:58,865 --> 00:12:00,226
[SPEAKER_01]: It will have to even itself up.

189
00:12:03,029 --> 00:12:04,550
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it has to, yeah, that's what it says.

190
00:12:04,570 --> 00:12:05,131
[SPEAKER_02]: Or stay steady.

191
00:12:05,611 --> 00:12:06,292
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what I mean.

192
00:12:06,332 --> 00:12:07,053
[SPEAKER_02]: Even itself up.

193
00:12:07,093 --> 00:12:08,114
[SPEAKER_02]: Evenly yoked.

194
00:12:08,134 --> 00:12:08,694
[SPEAKER_02]: Yoked together.

195
00:12:08,855 --> 00:12:09,055
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

196
00:12:09,075 --> 00:12:09,956
[SPEAKER_02]: Something possibly.

197
00:12:09,996 --> 00:12:11,817
[SPEAKER_00]: You're holding something and then it's... Yeah.

198
00:12:12,058 --> 00:12:13,439
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to keep... Sure.

199
00:12:13,559 --> 00:12:14,880
[SPEAKER_00]: To see what's going on.

200
00:12:15,020 --> 00:12:18,524
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, you don't have to...

201
00:12:18,724 --> 00:12:19,625
[SPEAKER_01]: It's calming.

202
00:12:19,805 --> 00:12:21,286
[SPEAKER_01]: It calms down, literally.

203
00:12:21,467 --> 00:12:21,727
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

204
00:12:25,894 --> 00:12:27,795
[SPEAKER_02]: So, yeah, so it's certainly me.

205
00:12:27,815 --> 00:12:35,257
[SPEAKER_02]: So, samatha is more of that kind of passive, not ignoring the background.

206
00:12:35,297 --> 00:12:42,600
[SPEAKER_02]: Passive, non-ignoring, not engaging in with the right order of things that you just discerned.

207
00:12:43,440 --> 00:12:45,261
[SPEAKER_02]: Which you couldn't do without the vipassana.

208
00:12:45,481 --> 00:12:45,821
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

209
00:12:46,141 --> 00:12:48,742
[SPEAKER_01]: And you wouldn't be discerning it if it wasn't there endurance.

210
00:12:48,762 --> 00:12:49,062
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

211
00:12:49,102 --> 00:12:51,303
[SPEAKER_01]: If you haven't recognized the degree of its endurance.

212
00:12:51,663 --> 00:12:53,264
[SPEAKER_01]: And samatha is not so much

213
00:12:54,575 --> 00:12:55,656
[SPEAKER_01]: You then doing it.

214
00:12:55,876 --> 00:12:56,116
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

215
00:12:56,136 --> 00:12:57,997
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more the fact that these things are jobs.

216
00:12:58,017 --> 00:13:01,960
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the perfect samatha that the Buddha described is the jhānas.

217
00:13:02,120 --> 00:13:02,320
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

218
00:13:02,340 --> 00:13:03,321
[SPEAKER_01]: The one that he always praised.

219
00:13:03,381 --> 00:13:05,122
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why there is no sense of I am in them.

220
00:13:05,342 --> 00:13:05,843
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

221
00:13:06,423 --> 00:13:13,147
[SPEAKER_01]: Even for somebody who is still not an arahant, that sense has to subside completely before you get to develop.

222
00:13:13,167 --> 00:13:13,348
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

223
00:13:13,368 --> 00:13:16,269
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can't be doing it and it's about you not doing it.

224
00:13:16,289 --> 00:13:19,792
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's so pleasant and sublime and so on.

225
00:13:19,872 --> 00:13:21,373
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it doesn't require effort.

226
00:13:24,728 --> 00:13:26,189
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it develops discernment.

227
00:13:26,329 --> 00:13:29,310
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, with right view, you get jhānas, you will be at least anāgāmī.

228
00:13:29,751 --> 00:13:30,671
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, you get jhānas.

229
00:13:30,691 --> 00:13:32,612
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, you develop your mind to the extent of jhāna.

230
00:13:33,873 --> 00:13:37,355
[SPEAKER_01]: You would be free from sensuality and probably free from conceit in no time.

231
00:13:40,016 --> 00:13:49,701
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's just becoming fully familiar with the... as in, as solid as a rock, steady, the clarity of things that you discern.

232
00:13:49,941 --> 00:13:51,422
[SPEAKER_01]: It just becomes your mode of being.

233
00:13:52,582 --> 00:13:58,025
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, it fully becomes apparent, clear, beyond doubt, that, oh, there is no room for any.

234
00:13:58,205 --> 00:14:01,126
[SPEAKER_01]: The slightest degree of me and I am, and me choosing to do this.

235
00:14:08,430 --> 00:14:11,852
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, whatever I do, these things are enjoying.

236
00:14:11,872 --> 00:14:16,054
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the sole reason that it felt like two different directions for you.

237
00:14:16,794 --> 00:14:18,695
[SPEAKER_01]: Even they were unified with the general context.

238
00:14:18,755 --> 00:14:21,957
[SPEAKER_01]: As I said, it wasn't as accurate enough, because you still had a trace of idea of...

239
00:14:22,801 --> 00:14:24,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, I apply it.

240
00:14:24,282 --> 00:14:26,163
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you don't apply it.

241
00:14:26,263 --> 00:14:28,624
[SPEAKER_01]: You applying it, it's a separate thing now.

242
00:14:28,704 --> 00:14:34,827
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you can take me applying something, as there is an image that is used for you, which gives the context of your application.

243
00:14:35,267 --> 00:14:40,790
[SPEAKER_01]: But your vipassana and samādhi are on the level of there is an image, not on the level of your application of it.

244
00:14:42,331 --> 00:14:50,495
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, no, you can't have too much of it, the right mindfulness, and it's a necessary basis for any form of application or discernment.

245
00:15:07,193 --> 00:15:15,158
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, I mean, I imagine most people think samatha and vipassana is coming a step backwards, but people think that they're techniques.

246
00:15:15,178 --> 00:15:16,579
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, so these two things I do.

247
00:15:20,076 --> 00:15:22,878
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you have none if you don't have the right view first.

248
00:15:23,699 --> 00:15:27,161
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, Samādhi to Sutta, many other suttas as well.

249
00:15:28,322 --> 00:15:33,526
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't possibly practice rightly without knowing what the right practice is.

250
00:15:33,746 --> 00:15:35,167
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just a contradiction in terms.

251
00:15:35,828 --> 00:15:37,749
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's such a hard thing for people to accept as well.

252
00:15:38,530 --> 00:15:38,790
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, yeah.

253
00:15:38,810 --> 00:15:41,652
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it feels like, oh, so whatever I've been doing means it doesn't count for anything.

254
00:15:41,672 --> 00:15:45,895
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you don't know what it... you will only know whether it counts for something or not once you get the right view.

255
00:15:46,456 --> 00:15:49,038
[SPEAKER_01]: In hindsight, you can see whether your practice is counted or not.

256
00:15:49,838 --> 00:15:57,804
[SPEAKER_01]: By you being too afraid to admit that it might not count for anything, it's basically what's going to hinder you from finding what does count for something.

257
00:15:59,025 --> 00:15:59,845
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just simply that.

258
00:16:00,806 --> 00:16:08,232
[SPEAKER_01]: Fear that you might be... Yeah, even if you wasted all of your time, 20 years of your supposed practice, it's never too late.

259
00:16:10,100 --> 00:16:12,001
[SPEAKER_01]: And if the practice was right, you have nothing to fear.

260
00:16:12,201 --> 00:16:14,201
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why are you fearing questioning it?

261
00:16:14,221 --> 00:16:15,782
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't lose from practicing more.

262
00:16:16,262 --> 00:16:16,602
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

263
00:16:16,742 --> 00:16:17,362
[SPEAKER_01]: What are you going to lose?

264
00:16:17,482 --> 00:16:24,525
[SPEAKER_01]: You can only lose from refraining to practice further because of fear of what you thought you invested in beforehand.

265
00:16:24,545 --> 00:16:28,046
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just, I think, for people, those people, it's less painful to...

266
00:16:28,206 --> 00:16:28,866
[SPEAKER_01]: It's less painful.

267
00:16:29,006 --> 00:16:36,088
[SPEAKER_01]: So, again, it boils down to the criteria that Buddha said Bhutujjana would only know, which is, this pleases me, and this thing displeases me.

268
00:16:36,248 --> 00:16:38,029
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why I follow it, that's why I reject it.

269
00:16:39,080 --> 00:16:45,322
[SPEAKER_01]: Like even, even intellect and reasoning are heavily rooted in pleasing, displeasing.

270
00:16:45,362 --> 00:16:47,223
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I'm for it, that's why I'm against it.

271
00:16:47,243 --> 00:16:51,224
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, that's why I find arguments for it, that's why I find arguments against it.

272
00:16:51,284 --> 00:16:52,464
[SPEAKER_01]: Just because it pleases me.

273
00:16:53,145 --> 00:16:54,405
[SPEAKER_01]: And they remain ignorant of that.

274
00:16:55,966 --> 00:16:57,326
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that's why they help abuse.

275
00:16:58,046 --> 00:17:00,667
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if you're following the right teaching, you're following for the wrong reasons.

276
00:17:01,287 --> 00:17:01,487
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

277
00:17:02,288 --> 00:17:02,548
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

